@@ -1,5 +1,8 @@                                                                                                      
 # Living Survey: cancer                                                                                             
                                                                                                                     
 ## Introduction                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                     
 This document collects the scientific literature regarding **cancer**.                                              
+                                                                                                                    
+## Diagnostic Imaging and AI-Driven Analysis                                                                        
+Recent advances in foundation models and computer vision have significantly enhanced cancer screening and diagnosis.
Foundation model embeddings of screening mammograms have been shown to encode pre-diagnostic tissue changes without  
task-specific adaptation, with malignant cases drifting faster along a "cancer direction" in embedding space than    
controls [^2609.26605]. Similarly, the Mammo-LIFE framework integrates longitudinal mammographic features with       
clinical variables to predict post-radiotherapy outcomes, utilizing low-rank adaptation and longitudinal comparison  
modules [^2609.26443]. In pathology, the WILSON vision-language foundation model represents whole-slide images as    
multi-magnification composites, outperforming dedicated case-level models and generating diagnostic text with high   
recall [^2609.25123]. For ultrasound analysis, FreqDINO++ introduces a frequency-guided multi-task routing adapter to
bridge the domain gap from natural images, achieving robust performance across 27 clinical tasks [^2609.20340].      
Furthermore, counterfactual explanations guided by Formal Concept Analysis (FCA-CF) have achieved perfect validity   
and emergent sparsity in multi-modal breast cancer diagnosis, offering clinically actionable insights where          
traditional attribution methods fail [^2609.20067]. Spatial transcriptomics benchmarking reveals that active learning
strategies for spot selection do not consistently outperform random sampling at small budgets, highlighting the      
complexity of high-dimensional continuous expression vectors [^2609.27208]. Microbiome-based cancer detection        
benchmarks (BreCol) demonstrate that classical models outperform deep learning approaches on holdout data,           
emphasizing the value of unsupervised clustering for compositional signals [^2609.27207]. Endoscopic imaging datasets
like ERCPMP-Gx link polyposis phenotypes to histopathology and germline findings, supporting AI-driven classification
of hereditary syndromes [^2609.20815]. Finally, hybrid quantum-classical models on the Breast Cancer Wisconsin       
dataset show that classical classifiers currently surpass quantum variants in accuracy, though quantum models exhibit
competitive benign-class recall [^2609.23476].                                                                       
+                                                                                                                    
+## Tumor Evolution and Microenvironment Dynamics                                                                    
+Understanding the physical and evolutionary mechanisms driving tumor progression is critical. Tumor-induced lipid   
loss physically remodels white adipose tissue, accelerating breast cancer invasion into mammary fat, a process       
exacerbated by obesity-associated tissue pressure [^2609.23229]. At the clonal level, mutation order and selection   
shape intratumor heterogeneity; a strong driver acquired early expands intermediate lineages, whereas late           
acquisition strengthens early-founded clones, potentially producing non-monotone relationships between selective     
advantage and clonal concentration [^2609.25838]. Mechanistic modeling of tumor growth using 2D reaction-diffusion   
equations allows for the estimation of cell diffusivity and proliferation rates from single-point biopsies, providing
potential biomarkers for radiotherapy risk-stratification [^2609.22363]. Additionally, non-parametric estimators for 
excess recurrent events extend relative survival analysis to recurrent hospitalizations, evaluated using rectal      
cancer data [^2609.26328].                                                                                           
+                                                                                                                    
+## Genomic, Proteomic, and Molecular Profiling                                                                      
+Multi-omics approaches are uncovering novel biomarkers and regulatory networks. A pan-cancer co-expression network  
analysis identified hub genes with potential for predicting drug responses [^10.5281_zenodo.2574643]. Single-cell and
spatial multi-omics revealed that ccRCC lung metastases harbor cancer-associated fibroblast lymphocyte exclusion     
sites forming confined tumor microenvironments [^10.5281_zenodo.18767278]. In glioblastoma, nucleosome repositioning 
in cell-free DNA (cfDNA) from liquid biopsies provides a non-invasive readout of disease state, with repeat-based    
profiling achieving high diagnostic accuracy [^10.5526_err-00042900]. The chromatin-associated DEK oncogene promotes 
metastatic melanoma by modulating chromatin accessibility and transcriptional reprogramming, regulated by            
CK2-mediated phosphorylation [^10.17638_03194520]. Deubiquitinating enzymes (DUBs) like USP42 regulate YAP/TAZ       
activity in mesothelioma and head and neck cancers, offering potential therapeutic targets [^10.17638_03193849].     
Proteome-wide mapping identified stable transcriptomic predictors of protein abundance in cancer cell lines,         
revealing context-specific gene features [^2609.24004]. Inferred hub miRNAs (HubmiRs) drive cross-task improvements  
in drug mechanistic modeling, outperforming transcription factor activity in perturbational drug modeling            
[^2609.21280]. Bayesian posterior learning of mixed graphical models applied to breast cancer gene expression reveals
meaningful dependencies between profiles and cancer subtypes [^2609.23857]. Hybrid quantum-classical attention       
mechanisms for histopathology-based molecular profiling show selective gains in data-limited cancer cohorts,         
redistributing predictive accuracy across biologically relevant targets [^2609.21115]. Single-cell spatial atlases of
pan-cancer metastatic brain tumors provide comprehensive multi-omic resources for studying tumor-immune ecosystems   
[^10.5281_zenodo.21816284][^10.5281_zenodo.21782885]. Peptide-HLA binding prediction for neoantigen identification   
benefits from hybrid quantum-classical neural networks that improve sample efficiency in low-data regimes            
[^2609.19642]. Prostate cancer screening pilot analysis using THz spectroscopy of urine vapors identifies candidate  
spectral features complementary to PSA testing [^2609.23814].                                                        
+                                                                                                                    
+## Therapeutic Strategies and Clinical Applications                                                                 
+Novel therapeutic frameworks and clinical trial methodologies are being optimized. A federated AI framework for     
pancreatic cancer treatment addresses GDPR boundaries and partially overlapping features across distributed sites,   
showing preliminary success in subtype identification [^2609.24718]. For esophageal cancer, an uncertainty-quantified
ensemble model predicts severe radiotherapy-induced immunosuppression (G4RIL), supporting personalized treatment     
decisions like proton therapy selection [^2609.25139]. In breast cancer, polyunsaturated fatty acids (PUFAs) mediate 
the antitumor effects of fasting by inducing lipid peroxidation and ferroptosis, supporting combined PUFA            
supplementation and cyclic fasting for TNBC [^10.21954_ou.ro.00108036]. Non-genotoxic topoisomerase II catalytic     
inhibitors have been developed and characterized for targeted cancer therapy [^10.14288_1.0451818]. Prostate cancer  
cells exhibit growth inhibition and lethality when treated with omega-3 fatty acids (EPA), which modulate            
mitochondrial succinylation networks [^10.17638_03195029]. Multi-omic prognostic modeling across 10,000 checkpoint   
blockade cases dissects tumor-immune ecosystems to identify prognostic cellular programs [^10.5281_zenodo.18706180]. 
Transportability methods combine routine care data with trial summary measures to estimate absolute treatment effects
on the treated (ATT) for breast cancer endocrine therapy [^2609.24305]. Structured screen-and-select variable        
selection (S3VS) improves variable recovery in ultra-high-dimensional ovarian cancer data by exploiting predictor    
dependence [^2609.24945].                                                                                            
+                                                                                                                    
+## Clinical Cohorts and Epidemiological Insights                                                                    
+Large-scale cohort studies and epidemiological analyses continue to inform cancer risk and management. Comprehensive
RNA-seq analysis of a Korean colorectal cancer cohort identified differentially expressed genes enriched in cell     
cycle and IL-17 pathways, alongside Consensus Molecular Subtype correlations [^10.5281_zenodo.8307739]. The built    
environment's association with cancer risk highlights the role of spatial and societal factors in oncology           
[^10.14288_1.0451801]. Long-term tail modeling in survival analysis via extended generalized Pareto distributions    
integrates extreme value theory with survival analysis, revealing trade-offs between flexibility and stability in    
tail-index estimation under censoring [^2609.28321].                                                                 
                                                                                                                     

