 ## Introduction                                                                                                     
 ## Diagnostic Imaging and AI-Driven Analysis                                                                        
 Recent advances in foundation models and computer vision have significantly enhanced cancer screening and diagnosis.
Foundation model embeddings of screening mammograms have been shown to encode pre-diagnostic tissue changes without  
task-specific adaptation, with malignant cases drifting faster along a "cancer direction" in embedding space than    
controls [^2609.26605]. Similarly, the Mammo-LIFE framework integrates longitudinal mammographic features with       
clinical variables to predict post-radiotherapy outcomes, utilizing low-rank adaptation and longitudinal comparison  
modules [^2609.26443]. In pathology, the WILSON vision-language foundation model represents whole-slide images as    
multi-magnification composites, outperforming dedicated case-level models and generating diagnostic text with high   
recall [^2609.25123]. For ultrasound analysis, FreqDINO++ introduces a frequency-guided multi-task routing adapter to
bridge the domain gap from natural images, achieving robust performance across 27 clinical tasks [^2609.20340].      
Furthermore, counterfactual explanations guided by Formal Concept Analysis (FCA-CF) have achieved perfect validity   
and emergent sparsity in multi-modal breast cancer diagnosis, offering clinically actionable insights where          
traditional attribution methods fail [^2609.20067]. Spatial transcriptomics benchmarking reveals that active learning
strategies for spot selection do not consistently outperform random sampling at small budgets, highlighting the      
complexity of high-dimensional continuous expression vectors [^2609.27208]. Microbiome-based cancer detection        
benchmarks (BreCol) demonstrate that classical models outperform deep learning approaches on holdout data,           
emphasizing the value of unsupervised clustering for compositional signals [^2609.27207]. Endoscopic imaging datasets
like ERCPMP-Gx link polyposis phenotypes to histopathology and germline findings, supporting AI-driven classification
of hereditary syndromes [^2609.20815]. Finally, hybrid quantum-classical models on the Breast Cancer Wisconsin       
benefits from hybrid quantum-classical neural networks that improve sample efficiency in low-data regimes            
[^2609.19642]. Prostate cancer screening pilot analysis using THz spectroscopy of urine vapors identifies candidate  
spectral features complementary to PSA testing [^2609.23814].                                                        
                                                                                                                     
